<p dir="auto"><strong>Medium Severity</strong></p>

<p dir="auto"><code class="notranslate">hasDatabaseURLSecret</code> uses <code class="notranslate">u.Query().Get("password")</code> which is case-sensitive, while the <code class="notranslate">databaseURLPattern</code> regex that feeds it is case-insensitive (<code class="notranslate">(?i)</code>). A database URL like <code class="notranslate">postgresql://host/db?user=svc&amp;Password=secret</code> would be matched by the pattern but <code class="notranslate">Get("password")</code> returns empty for the key <code class="notranslate">Password</code>. The three sibling detectors (<code class="notranslate">hasJDBCPassword</code>, <code class="notranslate">hasKeywordDSNPassword</code>, <code class="notranslate">hasSemicolonConnectionPassword</code>) all delegate to <code class="notranslate">hasNonPlaceholderPasswordAssignment</code> which uses the case-insensitive <code class="notranslate">passwordAssignmentRegex</code>, so they handle <code class="notranslate">Password</code>, <code class="notranslate">PASSWORD</code>, and <code class="notranslate">pwd</code> variants correctly. This inconsistency creates a credential-leak gap specific to non-JDBC database URLs with a non-lowercase <code class="notranslate">password</code> query parameter name.</p>



