content.text:
Because it parsed each JSONL line correctly, but then scanned each decoded string value in isolation.

For the working case, the decoded value is one string containing the whole key:

```text
key:
REDACTED"}
{"content":"payload-line-1"}
{"content":"payload-line-2"}
{"content":"-----END OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY-----"}
```

`JSONLContent` unmarshals line 1, calls `String("-----BEGIN OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY-----")`, then line 2, calls `String("payload-line-1")`, etc. Betterleaks’ private-key rule needs enough surrounding content to match a full private-key block, so no individual string had the whole pattern.

Entropy also did not reliably save us because the OpenSSH payload line can be long but structurally base64-ish, and depending on the exact line it may not cross the current entropy rule in the way we need. The begin/end markers are also low entropy, so entropy will not catch them.

The fix I added makes Entire not depend on seeing the full block: it redacts private-key begin/end markers and OpenSSH key payload fragments too.

